L000130	\begin{abstract}
L000131	We perform fully relativistic GRMHD simulations of magnetized, self-gravitating black hole–disk (BHD) systems
L000132	in which the black hole spin is misaligned with the disk angular momentum. Massive disks ({disk to BH} mass ratios
L000133	{of} $16-28\%$)  around rapidly rotating black holes ($\chi\lesssim 0.97$) develop a nonaxisymmetric instability
L000134	for tilt angles from $0^\circ$ to $180^\circ$. Magnetic stresses damp, but {do} not completely {suppress}, the
L000135	nonaxisymmetric instability, and {corresponding gravitational wave} (GW) emission, in aligned systems, while they
L000136	enhance it in antialigned BHDs:  MRI-driven turbulence enhances angular momentum transport and accelerates nonlinear
L000137	{instability} evolution in misaligned  configurations. All models launch magnetically driven jets consistent with the
L000138	Blandford–Znajek (BZ) mechanism,  with collimation depending on spin orientation. {The GWs reflect} strong nonaxisymmetric
L000139	structure from  {a} persistent $m=1$ mode.  The coupling between fast MRI and the slower nonaxisymmetric instability
L000140	{growth} governs the outcome, with tilt controlling how MRI modifies the global mode. These simulations provide the first
L000141	self-consistent GRMHD treatment of tilted, self-gravitating BHD systems and support their role as multimessenger sources.
L000142	\end{abstract}
L000143	
L000144	\maketitle
L000145	
L000146	
L000147	
L000148	
L000149	\textit{Introduction.}\textemdash
L000150	BHs across all mass scales are commonly surrounded by accretion disks formed in massive
L000151	star collapse~\cite{2009ARA&A..47...63S,OConnor:2010moj,Sun:2018gcl},
L000152	AGN activity~\cite{EventHorizonTelescope:2022wkp,EventHorizonTelescope:2022ago}, asymmetric
L000153	supernovae~\cite{Gottlieb:2022tkb,Siegel:2017nub}, and compact object mergers~\cite{KAGRA:2021vkt}.
L000154	These  systems are key multimessenger probes of strong gravity and high-energy
L000155	astrophysics~\cite{Leong:2025qiw}. A crucial parameter is the BH spin, which can
L000156	reach near-extreme values (e.g., $0.90\pm0.06$ for Sgr $\rm A^*$~\cite{Daly:2023axh}). When the
L000157	BH spin is misaligned with the disk angular momentum, the system dynamics and observables can
L000158	change substantially, affecting jet precession and power~\cite{Liska:2017alm,Jiang:2025xly}, accretion variability~\cite{Ingram:2019mna,Tsokaros:2022hjk}, and gravitational wave (GW) emission from
L000159	non-axisymmetric disk dynamics~\cite{Tsokaros:2022hjk}.
L000160	
L000161	The astrophysical relevance of spinning, misaligned BHs is highlighted by systems such as the blazar
L000162	OJ 287~\cite{2026A&A...705A..23G}. While its quasi-periodic outbursts have often been interpreted as
L000163	evidence for a supermassive BH binary, recent observations suggest alternative explanations involving
L000164	a single misaligned BHD~\cite{Butuzova:2020khp}. Large spin–orbit misalignments may arise through several
L000165	formation channels. In isolated binaries, natal supernova kicks can tilt the orbital plane~\cite{Hobbs:2005yx,
L000166	Kalogera:1999tq}. Gravitational wave (GW) observations indicate that a substantial fraction ($12$–$44\%$)
L000167	of BH binaries exhibit spin–orbit misalignments larger than $90^\circ$~\cite{LIGOScientific:2020kqk}. Such
L000168	processes can naturally produce tilted BHD systems, for example after BH–neutron star mergers in which
L000169	the disrupted neutron star forms a misaligned accretion disk around the remnant BH~\cite{Foucart:2010eq}.
L000170	The resulting geometry can strongly affect accretion, magnetic structure, jet stability,
L000171	and the associated electromagnetic (EM) and GW signals.
L000172	
L000173	BHD systems power magnetically driven relativistic jets from
L000174	short $\gamma$-ray bursts to AGN{s}~\cite{prs15,Ruiz:2016rai,2011IJMPD..20.1547R}. In the standard picture,
L000175	magnetic flux supplied by the accretion flow threads the BH and extracts its rotational energy to drive
L000176	collimated outflows. Recent GRMHD simulations~\cite{Liska:2019vne,Liska:2017alm,Cui:2024ggx} suggest
L000177	that strong BH spin–disk misalignment can modify this mechanism. Jet formation depends on the accumulation
L000178	of large-scale poloidal flux and its coupling to a tilted, precessing disk~\cite{0264-9381-27-8-084013,Etienne:2012te,Etienne:2013qia,Ruiz:2020via,Cui:2024ggx}. Large misalignments
L000179	can induce jet–disk interactions, magnetic
L000180	flux eruptions, and intermittent outflows. Previous studies typically neglected disk self-gravity and considered
L000181	low-mass disks  in fixed spacetimes~\cite{Liska:2017alm,Liska:2019vne,2007ApJ...668..417F}. {By contrast,
L000182	massive self-gravitating disks dynamically couple to the BH and modify the spacetime geometry. Although global
L000183	instabilities such as the Papaloizou–Pringle instability (PPI) can arise even in non-self-gravitating tori,
L000184	self-gravity can influence their growth and nonlinear evolution~\cite{Kiuchi2011b,Tsokaros:2022hjk,Wessel:2020hvu,
L000185	Wessel:2023jng}.} Understanding jet–disk interactions therefore requires a firm grasp of the underlying disk
L000186	dynamics  in tilted systems.
L000187	
L000188	The dynamics of tilted BHDs are {influenced} by the Lense–Thirring (LT) effect~\cite{1918PhyZ...19..156L}, in
L000189	which frame dragging from the spinning BH induces disk precession. In viscous disks this can lead to the
L000190	Bardeen–Petterson (BP) effect~\cite{1975ApJ...195L..65B}, where the inner disk aligns with the BH spin.
L000191	GR simulations of self-gravitating disks show instead that the disk and BH can undergo coupled precession
L000192	and nutation, with LT torques driving global disk precession that in turn induces BH
L000193	precession~\cite{Mewes:2015gma,Mewes:2016olz,Tsokaros:2022hjk}. Such disks may also develop the
L000194	non-axisymmetric $m=1$ PPI~\cite{1984MNRAS.208..721P}, which redistributes angular momentum and produces a
L000195	persistent asymmetric mass distribution, making these systems potential sources of quasi-periodic GWs~\cite{Wessel:2020hvu,Shibata:2021sau,Tsokaros:2022hjk,Wessel:2023jng}.
L000196	
L000197	Previous numerical studies have explored tilted disks in several regimes. Early GRMHD simulations of thick
L000198	disks~\cite{Fragile:2004gp} found global precession without BP alignment and revealed plunging streams connecting
L000199	the disk to the BH horizon. Later work on thinner disks identified disk tearing and standing shocks that enhance
L000200	angular momentum transport at large tilt angles~\cite{Liska:2019vne,White:2019udt}. Ray-tracing
L000201	calculations~\cite{2011ApJ...730...36D} further showed that the radiation edge of tilted disks can become largely
L000202	independent of BH spin, while strongly magnetized disks may exhibit magnetically driven retrograde
L000203	precession~\cite{Chatterjee:2023ber}. However, these studies neglected disk self-gravity and evolved low-mass
L000204	disks in fixed spacetimes. GRMHD simulations of massive self-gravitating BHDs have mostly focused on aligned systems,
L000205	where magnetic fields damp but do not suppress the PPI~\cite{Wessel:2023jng}. Highly spinning and tilted systems
L000206	have so far been explored only in hydrodynamic simulations~\cite{Mewes:2015gma,Mewes:2016olz,Tsokaros:2018zlf,Tsokaros:2022hjk}.
L000207	A fully self-consistent GRMHD treatment of massive, tilted, self-gravitating BHDs is therefore still lacking.
L000208	
L000209	We adopt units where $G=c=M_\odot =1$ throughout.
L000210	
L000211	
L000212	
L000213	
L000214	
L000215	\textit{Initial data and numerical methods.}\textemdash
L000216	
L000217	We consider the self-gravitating BHD models (A1–A4) previously reported in~\cite{Tsokaros:2022hjk}.
L000218	These equilibrium configurations were computed with the {\tt COCAL}  code using the methods
L000219	of~\cite{Tsokaros:2018zlf}, which implement the Komatsu–Eriguchi–Hachisu scheme~\cite{1989MNRAS.237..355K}
L000220	to construct initial data for massive, non-magnetized, self-gravitating tori. In all cases, the disk
L000221	angular momentum is initially aligned with the coordinate $z-$axis, while the near-extremal BH spin
L000222	$\chi = J_{\rm BH}/M_{\rm BH}^2 \sim 0.97$ is tilted by angles~$\theta_s=0^\circ$, $45^\circ$, $90^\circ$,
L000223	and $180^\circ$ (see the BH spin vectors in Fig. \ref{fig:ID_BNdisk}). The disk-to–BH mass ratios
L000224	satisfy $M_0/M_{\rm BH}=0.156$–$0.280$ (see Table I in~\cite{Tsokaros:2022hjk}). The disk is described
L000225	by a $\Gamma= 4/3$ polytrope  with {nearly} constant  specific angular momentum ($j\sim r^{0.01}$).
L000226	Here $M_0$ is the rest-mass of the disk, and~$J_{\rm BH}$ and $M_{\rm BH}$ are the quasi-local angular
L000227	momentum and the Christodoulou mass of  the  BH.
L000228	
L000229	We {now} seed the disk with an {initially weak} poloidal magnetic field~(see Fig.
L000230	\ref{fig:ID_BNdisk}) using the same procedure as in~\cite{Ruiz:2023hit}. To ensure the resulting BHD is
L000231	MRI-unstable, we set the initial magnetic-to-gas pressure ratio to $\beta^{-1}=0.01$. The self-gravitating
L000232	BHD models A1–A4 are evolved using the {\tt ILLINOIS~GRMHD} code~\cite{Etienne:2010ui}. It employs the BSSN
L000233	formulation of Einstein equations, integrated with a fourth-order Runge-Kutta scheme and sixth-order finite
L000234	differencing. Moving mesh refinement is implemented via the~{\tt Carpet}  infrastructure~\cite{Schnetter:2003rb}.
L000235	The hydrodynamics are evolved using high-resolution shock-capturing methods with an ideal gas $\Gamma-$law
L000236	equation of state (EoS; $P=(\Gamma-1)\rho_0\,\epsilon$) with $\Gamma=4/3$ {which, e.g., would be the case for
L000237	a radiation dominated pressure support}. {The magnetic field evolution is treated within the ideal GRMHD
L000238	approximation, assuming infinite conductivity and neglecting explicit resistive effects.}
L000239	Gauge conditions and constraint damping
L000240	are applied for numerical stability~\cite{Etienne:2011re}. All cases are evolved using 13 nested mesh refinement
L000241	boxes centered on the BH. The computational domain is $[-4000M_{\rm BH}, 4000M_{\rm BH}]^3$ with finest
L000242	resolution $\Delta x_{\rm min} = 50M_{\rm BH}/212 = 0.0122M_{BH}$. No symmetry assumptions are imposed.
L000243	
L000244	\begin{figure}
L000245	\includegraphics[width=0.98\columnwidth]{BHdisk_initial.png}
L000246	\caption{ Volume rendering of the {initial} BHD system threaded by a purely poloidal magnetic
L000247	field. The BH is depicted as a black spheroid. The yellow arrow shows the initial spin
L000248	direction for case A2 $(45^\circ)$, while the fainter arrows indicate the spin orientations
L000249	for other cases (see Table I in~\cite{Tsokaros:2022hjk}).}
L000250	\label{fig:ID_BNdisk}
L000251	\end{figure}
L000252	
L000253	
L000254	
L000255	
L000256	
L000257	\textit{Results.}\textemdash
L000258	
L000259	The final outcome of the GRMHD simulations of models A1–A4 is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:final_outcome}.
L000260	In all cases the BHD launches a magnetically driven, collimated outflow from the BH poles, surrounded
L000261	by a turbulent, tilted disk whose magnetic field organizes into a jet-like structure.
L000262	Fig.~\ref{fig:A2_b2_over_2rho} shows a representative tilted case (A2), highlighting
L000263	magnetically-dominated regions and horizon-anchored field lines consistent with flux accumulation on the BH.
L000264	
L000265	
L000266	
L000267	
L000268	
L000269	\begin{figure*}
L000270	
L000271	\begin{turn}{90}
L000272	\hspace{1.7cm}\bf A1: Tilted $0^\circ$
L000273	\end{turn}
L000274	
L000275	\includegraphics[width=0.98\columnwidth]{0_vel.png}
L000276	
L000277	\includegraphics[width=0.98\columnwidth]{45_vel.png}
L000278	\begin{turn}{90}
L000279	\hspace{1.7cm}\bf A2: Tilted $45^\circ$
L000280	\end{turn}
L000281	
L000282	\begin{turn}{90}
L000283	\hspace{1.7cm} \bf A3: Tilted $90^\circ$
L000284	\end{turn}
L000285	\includegraphics[width=0.98\columnwidth]{pvel.png}
L000286	\includegraphics[width=0.98\columnwidth]{180_vel.png}
L000287	\begin{turn}{90}
L000288	\hspace{1.7cm} \bf A4: Tilted $180^\circ$
L000289	\end{turn}
L000290	\caption{Volume rendering of the rest-mass density $\rho_0$ for the final outcome
L000291	of BHDs (see Table I in~\cite{Tsokaros:2022hjk}), normalized to its initial maximum value
L000292	(log scale). In all cases, a magnetically-driven, collimated outflow (jet) emerges along the
L000293	BH polar regions. Arrows denote fluid velocities  while white lines trace the magnetic field,
L000294	illustrating its collimation into a jet-like structure. Here {the period} $P_c/M=\{370,404,357,422\}$.}
L000295	\label{fig:final_outcome}
L000296	\end{figure*}
L000297	
L000298	These simulations represent the magnetized counterparts to the hydrodynamic models
L000299	of~\cite{Tsokaros:2022hjk}. Fig.~\ref{fig:modes} compares the non-axisymmetric mode
L000300	amplitudes $C_m=\int \rho_0 u^t\sqrt{-g}e^{im\phi}d^3x$~\cite{Wessel:2020hvu} for magnetized
L000301	(solid) and hydrodynamic (dashed) evolutions. All configurations remain unstable to the PPI,
L000302	with the antialigned case (A4) showing the most violent evolution, while the aligned case (A1)
L000303	exhibits the weakest instability, with $m=1$ amplitudes about an order of magnitude smaller
L000304	than in the hydrodynamic counterpart.
L000305	
L000306	Magnetic turbulence is known to damp the $m=1$ mode in aligned BHD systems~\cite{Wessel:2023jng}.
L000307	Unlike that study, where magnetization was introduced after PPI saturation,
L000308	our simulations include magnetic fields from the start, allowing MRI and PPI to develop simultaneously.
L000309	Significant damping occurs only in the aligned configuration (A1), where the growth rate is $\sim30\%$
L000310	lower than in the hydrodynamic model (Table~\ref{tab:mode_growth}). This weaker suppression suggests
L000311	that coherent hydrodynamic structures can emerge from MRI-turbulent flows and dominate the dynamics,
L000312	whether through standing shocks in tilted disks~\cite{White:2019udt} or through the PPI in our simulations.
L000313	
L000314	\setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
L000315	\begin{table}
L000316	\caption{Mode growth and pattern speed for the $m=1$ mode~\cite{Tsokaros:2022hjk}.}
L000317	\label{tab:mode_growth}
L000318	\begin{tabular}{cccccccccccccc}
L000319	\hline\hline
L000320	Model & ${\rm Im}(\GO_1)/\Omega_c$  & ${\rm Im}(\GO_1)/\Omega_c$ & $\Omega_{p,1}/\Omega_c$ & $\Omega_{p,1}/\Omega_c$ \\
L000321	      &    hyd                      &     mag                    &             hyd          &    mag       \\ \hline\hline
L000322	A1    & $0.318$                     &0.227                       & $0.748$                 &  $0.448$   \\ \hline
L000323	A2    & $0.177$                     &0.265                       & $0.748$                 &  $0.398$   \\ \hline
L000324	A3    & $0.177$                     &0.265                       & $0.637$                 &  $0.374$   \\ \hline
L000325	A4    & $0.227$                     &3.183                       & $0.812$                 &  $0.048$   \\ \hline
L000326	\end{tabular}                                                                                                                                            \end{table}
L000327	
L000328	In tilted configurations the PPI growth depends strongly on BH spin orientation: magnetized misaligned
L000329	cases show a $\gtrsim50\%$ enhancement relative to the hydrodynamic models. As in~\cite{Tsokaros:2022hjk},
L000330	the antialigned case (A4) is the most unstable. This contrasts with thin, non-self-gravitating tilted
L000331	disks~\cite{Liska:2019vne}, where large tilts produce disk tearing rather than stronger global modes.
L000332	In our self-gravitating disks the PPI dominates even at high tilts, indicating that disk mass plays a
L000333	key role in determining the governing instability. The $\sim 10-${fold} increase in growth rate for magnetized
L000334	A4 relative to its hydrodynamic counterpart further shows that MRI-driven transport combined with tilt
L000335	destabilizes the disk more effectively than either effect alone.
L000336	
L000337	Magnetic turbulence triggers massive accretion on a timescale of $\sim 1\,P_c$ (see Supplemental Material),
L000338	compared with $\sim 6\,P_c$ in the hydrodynamic case. {Here $P_c$ is the orbital period at the disk
L000339	radius of maximum rest-mass density}. In magnetized A4 the growth rate is amplified by a
L000340	factor of $\sim 10$ (Table~\ref{tab:mode_growth}), producing rapid BH mass increase and spin-down to
L000341	$\sim 0.5$ within $\sim 1\,P_c$. The hierarchy between MRI and PPI timescales explains this behavior.
L000342	Our disks have nearly constant specific angular momentum ($j\sim r^{0.01}$), implying a shear parameter
L000343	$q\sim2$. The MRI growth rate $\sigma_{\rm MRI}\sim\Omega_c$ yields $\tau_{\rm MRI}\approx 0.15P_c$,
L000344	much shorter than the hydrodynamic PPI timescale ($\tau_{\rm PPI}\sim0.5$–$0.9P_c$)~\cite{Tsokaros:2022hjk}.
L000345	MRI turbulence develops first, creating an effectively viscous background. In misaligned systems
L000346	this turbulence enhances angular momentum transport and accelerates PPI growth, leading to stronger disk
L000347	compression and earlier accretion.
L000348	
L000349	
L000350	
L000351	
L000352	
L000353	\begin{figure}
L000354	\begin{center}
L000355	\includegraphics[width=0.98\columnwidth]{b2_over_rho_45.png}
L000356	\caption{Volume rendering of the rest-mass density $\rho_0$ in the disk ({color bar same as in Fig.~\ref{fig:final_outcome}}),
L000357	and magnetization $B^2/(8\pi\rho_0)$ (log scale) in the funnel region near the end of simulation A2. White field lines mark regions
L000358	with $B^2/(8\pi\rho_0)\gtrsim10^{-2}$. The inset  shows horizon-threading field lines, magnetically dominated
L000359	polar regions, and inner-disk alignment with the BH spin.}
L000360	\label{fig:A2_b2_over_2rho}
L000361	\end{center}
L000362	\end{figure}
L000363	
L000364	
L000365	The pattern speed $\Omega_{p,1}$ (Eq.~11 in~\cite{Tsokaros:2022hjk}) is systematically lower in the magnetized
L000366	models (Table~\ref{tab:mode_growth}), and most pronounced in the antialigned case (A4) where it is $\sim 16$
L000367	times smaller than in the hydrodynamic counterpart. This reduction likely reflects MRI-driven angular momentum
L000368	redistribution that alters the disk structure and shifts the characteristic radius of the global $m=1$ mode.
L000369	The effect is strongest in tilted and antialigned systems where enhanced accretion and disk deformation
L000370	accompany the instability.
L000371	
L000372	Fig.~\ref{fig:J_BH} shows the evolution of the BH angular momentum components for
L000373	tilted configurations A2 and A3. Magnetic stresses modify the nearly conservative
L000374	precession seen in the hydrodynamic cases~\cite{Tsokaros:2022hjk}, introducing
L000375	torque-driven exchange between the BH and disk. In A2 the magnetized evolution exhibits
L000376	smoother oscillations and gradual alignment of the spin with the disk angular momentum,
L000377	while the spin magnitude remains nearly constant as $J_{\rm BH}$ and $M_{\rm BH}$ grow
L000378	through accretion. This behavior reflects MRI-driven angular momentum redistribution and
L000379	nonconservative BH–disk coupling. The gradual spin reorientation observed in A2–A3 differs
L000380	from {non-self-gravitating} thick-disk simulations~\cite{2007ApJ...668..417F},  where global precession dominated
L000381	with little alignment, likely reflecting the dynamical spacetime and stronger BHD
L000382	angular-momentum exchange enabled by the self-gravitating disk in our models. The smoother
L000383	evolution of magnetized simulations relative to hydrodynamic ones~\cite{Tsokaros:2022hjk}
L000384	also contrasts with magnetically arrested disk (MAD) simulations~\cite{Chatterjee:2023ber},
L000385	where strong magnetic torques can drive retrograde precession that overwhelms LT  torques.
L000386	Our models instead remain in the standard-and-normal-evolution (SANE) regime ($\beta\sim100$),
L000387	where MRI-driven turbulence transports angular momentum but does not magnetically arrest
L000388	the flow, leaving the dynamics LT-dominated. The rapid spin-down in A4 provides an alternative
L000389	pathway to similar outcomes: here angular-momentum cancellation during MRI-enhanced accretion
L000390	reduces the BH spin through a burst of accretion rather than sustained magnetic torques.
L000391	
L000392	
L000393	The differences are more pronounced in the $90^\circ$ configuration (A3). The hydrodynamic
L000394	evolution shows an abrupt transition near $t\sim 3P_c$ associated with nonlinear PPI growth
L000395	and a burst of accretion that reduces the spin to $\chi\sim0.85$. In contrast, the magnetized
L000396	case evolves more smoothly: continuous angular momentum accretion gradually increases $J_{\rm BH}$
L000397	and produces a final spin $\chi\sim0.98$, while the spin vector slowly reorients toward the disk
L000398	axis (see Supplemental Material). This secular behavior is consistent with sustained MRI-driven
L000399	transport  and continuous accretion in tilted disks, which can gradually modify the BH spin magnitude
L000400	and promote alignment with the disk angular momentum axis~\cite{2007ApJ...668..417F,Liska:2017alm}.
L000401	
L000402	In the antialigned case (A4) magnetization produces qualitatively different behavior. MRI-driven
L000403	turbulence triggers an early, intense accretion episode that rapidly reduces the BH spin to
L000404	$\chi\sim0.5$ within $\sim1P_c$, {without leading to a spin-flip}. Here the spin evolution is
L000405	dominated by angular momentum cancellation  during accretion rather than by gradual alignment or
L000406	precessional dynamics.
L000407	
L000408	
L000409	
L000410	
L000411	
