L000450	\caption{Time evolution of the BH angular momentum components $J_i^{\rm BH}$ (colored) and
L000451	magnitude $|J^{\rm BH}|$ (grey) for tilted cases A2 ($45^\circ$; top) and A3 ($90^\circ$; bottom).}
L000452	\label{fig:J_BH}
L000453	\end{center}
L000454	\end{figure}
L000455	
L000456	The inset of Fig.~\ref{fig:PLuinosity} shows that the dynamical ejecta masses are similar in most
L000457	cases ($\gtrsim0.03M_0$), but increase by a factor of $\sim8$ in the antialigned configuration. This
L000458	enhancement reflects the stronger accretion-driven outflows triggered by MRI turbulence. For
L000459	stellar-mass BHs such ejecta ($>0.01M_\odot$) could power kilonovae comparable to
L000460	GW170817~\cite{Cowperthwaite:2017dyu}, while in supermassive systems they may produce transient EM
L000461	signatures such as AGN-like flares or tidal disruption event afterglows~\cite{Horesh:2021gvp}.
L000462	
L000463	Fig.~\ref{fig:GWamplitude} shows the GW strain for the $l=m=2$ (top) and $l=2,m=1$ (bottom) modes for
L000464	representative tilted configurations A3 (left) and A4 (right), comparing magnetized evolutions (solid lines)
L000465	with their hydrodynamic counterparts (dashed lines). In aligned and moderately tilted systems ($\leq 90^\circ$),
L000466	the magnetized waveforms have systematically smaller amplitudes, reflecting the damping of large-scale
L000467	nonaxisymmetric modes by MRI-driven turbulence. However, in the antialigned case (A4), the magnetized evolution
L000468	produces larger amplitudes than the hydrodynamic case due to the violent instability and rapid MRI-triggered
L000469	accretion. This trend is also visible in the GW spectra (see Supplemental Material). Overall, magnetic fields
L000470	damp, but not completely suppress, GW emission in aligned systems but enhance it in antialigned BHDs.
L000471	
L000472	
L000473	
L000474	
L000475	\textit{Discussion.}\textemdash
L000476	
L000477	Magnetic fields do not suppress the PPI in massive, self-gravitating, tilted BHDs.
L000478	The instability persists in all configurations but develops earlier in misaligned
L000479	systems, where MRI-driven turbulence enhances angular momentum transport. The tilt
L000480	between the BH spin and disk angular momentum regulates the outcome: small tilts damp
L000481	the nonaxisymmetric mode, near-orthogonal configurations ($\sim90^\circ$) recover
L000482	amplitudes comparable to hydrodynamic evolutions, and antialigned systems ($>90^\circ$)
L000483	strongly amplify it. In the $180^\circ$ case the $m=1$ growth rate increases by $\sim10$
L000484	relative to the hydrodynamic counterpart, triggering rapid accretion and spin-down.
L000485	Magnetic stresses also introduce nonconservative BH–disk coupling, producing gradual
L000486	spin reorientation in A2 and A3 and strong spin reduction only in the antialigned configuration.
L000487	
L000488	All models launch magnetically dominated, horizon-threading outflows aligned with the BH
L000489	spin, consistent with the BZ mechanism. Misalignment does not prevent jet formation but
L000490	affects collimation and variability, with the strongest early EM luminosity occurring in
L000491	the antialigned case during rapid accretion. In contrast to fixed-background studies that
L000492	neglect disk self-gravity~\cite{Liska:2017alm,Liska:2019vne,2007ApJ...668..417F}, the global
L000493	$m=1$ mode and disk warping in our simulations do not disrupt jet launching. The GW signal
L000494	retains significant power beyond the $l=m=2$ mode. Magnetic turbulence generally reduces
L000495	coherent GW amplitudes, except in the antialigned configuration where enhanced instability
L000496	strengthens the signal.
L000497	
L000498	{Because the GRMHD equations with a $\Gamma$-law EoS are scale free in geometrized
L000499	units, each simulation represents a family of BH+disk systems sharing the same
L000500	dimensionless parameters. The choice $\Gamma=4/3$ is adopted here as an effective
L000501	description of the large-scale dynamics rather than a detailed microphysical EoS.
L000502	We note, however, that for supermassive BHs, the inner regions
L000503	of the disk are likely dominated by thermal radiation pressure,  for which
L000504	$\Gamma=4/3$ is a reasonable choice.}
L000505	{It is important to note that while very massive disks with $M_{\rm disk}/M_{\rm BH}\sim0.1$
L000506	may not be typical of standard AGN accretion flows, self-gravitating disks of this kind can
L000507	arise transiently in several astrophysical scenarios, including compact-object
L000508	mergers, collapsars, and some tidal disruption events (see e.g.~\cite{1993ApJ...405..273W,
L000509	  Goodman:2002gv,2020SSRv..216...35S}).
L000510	In addition, the formation scenario of supermassive BHs in the
L000511	early universe is unknown, and they could very well arise in gaseous regions whereby self-gravity
L000512	in the remnant disks is important and the pressure is radiation dominated.
L000513	Our $\Gamma=4/3$ thermal EoS is appropriate for such disk systems.
L000514	More generally, the present work is intended to
L000515	isolate the fundamental nonlinear interplay among self-gravity, magnetic fields, and tilt
L000516	in relativistic BH+disk systems.}
