L000927	(see~Fig.~4 in manuscript). This behavior is consistent with the hydrodynamic
L000928	trends reported in~\cite{Tsokaros:2022hjk}, where tilt influences the GW emission, while here magnetic
L000929	fields modulate the amplitude through damping or enhancement depending on the alignment configuration.
L000930	The $\sim 2$ higher amplitude in magnetized A4 relative to its hydrodynamic counterpart indicates that
L000931	MRI-triggered accretion can boost GW emission beyond hydrodynamic predictions.
L000932	
L000933	As in the hydrodynamic models, the characteristic strain peaks at approximately twice the orbital frequency
L000934	$f_c$ for A1 and A3, while A2 displays a primary peak near $3f_c$ with a secondary at $2f_c$. Our simulations
L000935	capture the early-time spectral structure of the signal, including amplitude and frequency modulations visible
L000936	for $t_{\rm ret} \lesssim 2 P_c$, associated with the initial nonlinear {instability} development of the disk. At later
L000937	times, these modulations diminish and the emission approaches a more quasimonochromatic signal, dominated
L000938	by the persistent $m=1$ mode. These results indicate that BHD systems across a wide range of mass
L000939	scales may constitute promising targets for future GW observations, while magnetic fields play a key role
L000940	in modulating their detectability by either suppressing or enhancing the GW emission depending on the alignment
